# Amendment cycle — telegram-interface-framework-integration (#9,
# 2026-04-22, ninth amendment): the telegram-interface component shipped
# as a standalone adapter-pattern consumer of the Claude MCP Telegram
# plugin, but no pos-v2 workspace composed it into its boot bundle —
# so first-run scaffolds wrote a 12-entry `bootstrap.yaml` that silently
# omitted the Telegram channel. Amendment #9 makes telegram-interface
# the thirteenth foundational adapter in workspace-bootstrap's bundle:
#   - New adapter `workspace_bootstrap.adapters.telegram_interface.
#     TelegramInterfaceContribution` constructs a `TelegramAdapter`
#     from telegram-interface's public surface (`AvailabilityProbe`,
#     `AccessFile.load`, `TelegramAdapter.build_channel`) and publishes
#     it on the host as `host.telegram_adapter`, `host.telegram_channel`,
#     `host.channel_registry["telegram"]`. Phase `after_orchestrator_
#     ready`, `after=("primary_persona","safety_layer")`, `required=
#     False` — the ordering slot matches self-correction's pattern.
#   - `_BOOTSTRAP_YAML` grows by one stanza (13 entries now); the
#     header-comment and `CONFIRMATION_SENTENCE` both advance "twelve"
#     → "thirteen" in lockstep.
#   - New `_TELEGRAM_YAML` starter + `"telegram.yaml"` entry in
#     `_SCAFFOLD_FILES` — the scaffold writes the per-workspace
#     config starter (owner ruling on proposal §5 #4).
#   - Fail-closed direction per proposal §2: at default (no
#     telegram.yaml, no token, no allowlist) the adapter composes
#     degraded-alive — `is_active=False`, `send` routes through the
#     fallback, no exception surfaces. `required: true` in
#     `~/.pos/telegram.yaml` flips the posture to fail-close, wrapping
#     the component's -32102 (IPC_TELEGRAM_TOKEN_INVALID) or -32108
